                        <h3 class="blog_heading">4. How long does laser hair removal take to see results? What happens after the first laser hair removal session? How many sessions are needed?</h3>
                        <p>Laser hair removal works when the hair is in its active growth rate. Different parts of the body have different growth cycles and growth rates, and therefore it could take longer or shorter depending on which area you're treating.</p>
                        <p>You'll see results after your first session, but it'll take multiple sessions to permanently remove hair. You may notice reduced hair in the first session itself and in four to six sessions, you'll see 80 to 85 percent less hair growth. Opt for maintenance treatment once a year for the best results.</p>
                        <p>Now, let’s talk about the cons of laser hair removal. It is a long process and it takes multiple sessions to see real results. Considering multiple sessions are often needed to fully stop hair growth, it is an expensive treatment. Even though it’s uncommon but few people develop blistering, discoloration, redness or swelling at the site.</p>
                        <p>By following your doctor’s instructions carefully, you will significantly reduce the risk of experiencing side effects. A few crucial points to take care of are: avoid sun exposure, waxing, and chemical peel in between the sessions.</p>
                         
                        <h3 class="blog_heading">5. How permanent is laser hair removal? Does laser hair removal last forever?</h3>
                        <p>On contrary to popular belief laser treatment does not have a permanent effect. But effects are close to permanent and long-lasting compared to any traditional method.</p>
                        <p>Here is how the process works. We know that the laser process targets hair follicles to prevent new growth of hairs. The process thus slows the hair growth for a longer period of time and even if hair grows after that, they are finer and fewer in number. The re-growth pattern depends on numerous factors, including the type of hair and skill of your dermatologist. So, consult a well-experienced person for better results. To summarize, laser treatment is not exactly permanent but effects last for an extended period of time than any other traditional method.</p>
                        <p>You will be required to undergo multiple sessions in order to see the best results. The number of sessions varies from person to another but mainly depends upon your skin tone, hair color, hair thickness, and body part. After each session, you’ll notice lesser hairs.</p>
                        
                        <h4 class="blog_heading">6. What are the Types of Laser Hair Removal Procedures?</h4>
                        <p>There are a few main types of powerful and versatile lasers used in hair removal. Each of the different types of lasers has a specific range of utility, depending on its wavelength and penetration:</p>
                        <ul class="disc_list">
                            <li><b>Alexandrite laser:</b> This type of laser uses light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation. It uses a laser that emits high energy light through an alexandrite crystal. It has a fast repetition rate and is most suitable for covering larger body parts such as legs,  and back. For those with light olive skin, the Alexandrite laser is a perfect solution.</li>
                            <li><b>Nd YAG:</b> The ND YAG laser is the preferred laser for dark skin. The main reason is that this laser has a weaker absorption rate other than lasers. This particular laser has a larger spot size and a high repetition rate, making it suitable for dark skin. When compared to other types of lasers it is less effective for light or fine hair.</li>
                            <li><b>Diode:</b> The diode laser uses semiconductor technology and a range of wavelengths. If you've got a pale-to-medium skin tone diode is the best solution for you. It means that they will not burn the darker skin while targeting hair follicles.</li>
                            <li><b>Ruby:</b> This is the first and oldest type of laser that uses a synthetic ruby crystal as its laser medium. It works best on for light and fine hair types and can cause discoloration on darker skin making it suitable for only fair skin.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Considering your skin type, hair color, target area your technician will choose the right laser according to your needs.</p>
                        
